{
 "category": "33-nonparametric-semiparametric",
 "cards": [
  {
   "id": 283,
   "method": "Kernel regression: Nadaraya-Watson, local linear and local polynomial with mixed data",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/kernel_regression.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_kernel_regress",
    "np_local_polynomial"
   ],
   "when": "estimating a conditional mean without committing to a functional form, when the shape of the relationship is the object of interest",
   "when_not": "the sample is small or the covariate dimension above three or four -- the curse of dimensionality makes the estimate noise; you need parameters to interpret",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "local linear",
     "criterion": "the default: no boundary bias, unlike Nadaraya-Watson"
    },
    {
     "alt": "series/sieve estimation",
     "criterion": "you want a basis expansion you can put in a regression"
    },
    {
     "alt": "GAM with penalised splines",
     "criterion": "several covariates, additive structure acceptable"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "fitted: the estimated conditional mean on the evaluation grid",
    "bandwidth: the bandwidth actually used, per covariate",
    "conf_band: pointwise band, which is not a uniform band",
    "r_squared: the nonparametric goodness of fit"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "multi-line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "Nadaraya-Watson is badly biased at the BOUNDARY of the support; local linear is not, which is why it is the default",
    "the pointwise band does not cover the whole curve simultaneously -- reading it as a uniform band overstates precision",
    "bandwidth choice dominates every other decision here; a cross-validated bandwidth on dependent data is too small"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Nadaraya 1964 (Theory of Probability and Its Applications 9:141); Watson 1964 (Sankhya A 26:359)",
    "Fan & Gijbels 1996, Local Polynomial Modelling and Its Applications",
    "Li & Racine 2007, Nonparametric Econometrics",
    "statsmodels.nonparametric.kernel_regression"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 284,
   "method": "Bandwidth selection: cross-validation, plug-in and rule of thumb",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/bandwidth_selection.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_bandwidth_select"
   ],
   "when": "the bandwidth is the single choice that determines a nonparametric estimate, and it should be made by a stated rule rather than by eye",
   "when_not": "the estimator supplies its own bandwidth internally and you have no reason to override it",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "least-squares cross-validation",
     "criterion": "the target is the regression function"
    },
    {
     "alt": "plug-in",
     "criterion": "you want speed and the density is roughly smooth"
    },
    {
     "alt": "rule of thumb",
     "criterion": "a quick first look only -- it assumes near-normality"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "bandwidth: the selected value per covariate",
    "criterion_curve: the objective over the searched grid, so a flat or multi-modal objective is visible",
    "method: which rule produced the answer"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "cross-validation on SERIALLY DEPENDENT data selects a bandwidth that is far too small; use a blocked or modified criterion for time series",
    "the rule of thumb assumes approximate normality and is badly wrong for skewed or multimodal data",
    "a flat criterion curve means the bandwidth is barely identified and the estimate is unstable across near-equivalent choices"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Hall, Racine & Li 2004 (JASA 99:1015)",
    "Sheather & Jones 1991 (JRSS-B 53:683)",
    "Silverman 1986, Density Estimation for Statistics and Data Analysis"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 285,
   "method": "Multivariate and conditional kernel density estimation",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/kernel_density.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_kde",
    "np_conditional_kde",
    "np_ecdf_grid"
   ],
   "when": "estimating a joint or conditional density without a parametric family -- for distributional questions, mode finding and density-based diagnostics",
   "when_not": "you need tail probabilities far beyond the data (-> extreme value theory); the dimension is high",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "univariate KDE",
     "criterion": "one variable and you only need a shape"
    },
    {
     "alt": "conditional KDE",
     "criterion": "the object is the distribution of y given x, not the joint"
    },
    {
     "alt": "mixture models",
     "criterion": "you want interpretable components rather than a smooth surface"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "density: the estimated density on the evaluation grid",
    "bandwidth: bandwidth per dimension",
    "cdf: the integrated density where requested",
    "loglike_cv: cross-validated log-likelihood, for comparing bandwidths"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "KDE has poor tail behaviour by construction; it must not be used for extreme quantiles",
    "in more than two or three dimensions the estimate is dominated by sparsity, whatever the bandwidth",
    "boundary bias is severe for variables with a hard bound such as durations or shares -- reflect or transform first"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Silverman 1986, Density Estimation for Statistics and Data Analysis",
    "Li & Racine 2007, Nonparametric Econometrics, ch.1-3",
    "statsmodels.nonparametric.kernel_density"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 286,
   "method": "Nonparametric conditional quantiles",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/conditional_quantiles.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_quantile_forest",
    "np_quantile_predict"
   ],
   "when": "the whole conditional distribution matters, not the mean, and you are unwilling to impose linearity on the quantile function",
   "when_not": "a linear quantile regression is adequate; the sample is too small for tail quantiles to be estimable",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "linear quantile regression",
     "criterion": "linearity is acceptable and you want coefficients"
    },
    {
     "alt": "quantile forests",
     "criterion": "many covariates and interactions"
    },
    {
     "alt": "distributional regression",
     "criterion": "you want a full parametric conditional distribution"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "quantiles: the estimated conditional quantile per requested level",
    "crossing_detected: whether the fitted quantile curves cross, which is a specification failure",
    "prediction_interval: the interval implied by the requested quantile pair"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "multi-line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "separately estimated quantile curves CAN CROSS, which is logically impossible for a distribution function -- check the flag rather than assuming it away",
    "extreme quantiles need far more data than central ones; a 1% conditional quantile from a few hundred observations is noise",
    "quantile forests give conditional quantiles under an implicit smoothness assumption from the tree structure, not a distribution-free guarantee"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Koenker & Bassett 1978 (Econometrica 46:33)",
    "Meinshausen 2006 (Journal of Machine Learning Research 7:983)",
    "quantile-forest"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 287,
   "method": "Series and sieve estimation with polynomial and spline bases",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/series_sieve.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_basis",
    "np_series_regress"
   ],
   "when": "you want nonparametric flexibility inside an ordinary regression, so the estimate has closed-form inference and composes with instrumental variables",
   "when_not": "the basis dimension needed exceeds what the sample supports; the relationship is genuinely local (-> kernel methods)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "kernel regression",
     "criterion": "you want a local estimate rather than a global basis"
    },
    {
     "alt": "penalised splines",
     "criterion": "you want smoothness chosen by a penalty rather than by basis size"
    },
    {
     "alt": "polynomial basis",
     "criterion": "the function is smooth and low order suffices"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "fitted: the fitted conditional mean",
    "basis: the constructed design matrix, reusable in any downstream regression",
    "k_selected: the basis dimension chosen and the criterion that chose it",
    "params, bse: coefficients on the basis, which have no direct interpretation"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "basis coefficients are NOT interpretable individually -- only the fitted function and its derivatives are",
    "high-order global polynomials oscillate wildly near the boundary (Runge's phenomenon); splines are the fix, not a higher degree",
    "the basis dimension must grow with the sample for consistency, and selecting it on the same data invalidates the naive standard error"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Newey 1997 (Journal of Econometrics 79:147-168)",
    "Chen 2007, Large sample sieve estimation of semi-nonparametric models (Handbook of Econometrics 6B)",
    "formulaic documentation"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 288,
   "method": "Partially linear models by the Robinson double-residual method",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/partially_linear.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_partially_linear"
   ],
   "when": "one coefficient is the object of interest and everything else is a nuisance you would rather not specify -- the semiparametric workhorse behind modern double machine learning",
   "when_not": "every covariate is of interest; the nuisance dimension is small enough for a fully parametric model",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "double machine learning",
     "criterion": "the nuisance is high dimensional and you want machine learners with cross-fitting"
    },
    {
     "alt": "fully parametric regression",
     "criterion": "the nuisance functional form is known"
    },
    {
     "alt": "GAM",
     "criterion": "you want each nuisance term visible and additive"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "theta: the parametric coefficient of interest, root-n consistent",
    "se, conf_int: inference that already accounts for the nuisance estimation",
    "nuisance_r2: fit of the two first-stage regressions",
    "n_folds: the cross-fitting used, which is what makes the inference valid"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "WITHOUT cross-fitting the estimate is biased by overfitting in the nuisance step; a single-fold implementation is not the same estimator",
    "the double-residual construction requires BOTH first stages to converge fast enough; a badly fitting nuisance model breaks the root-n claim silently",
    "theta is a partial effect holding the nuisance function fixed -- it is not a total derivative"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Robinson 1988 (Econometrica 56:931-954)",
    "Chernozhukov, Chetverikov, Demirer, Duflo, Hansen, Newey & Robins 2018 (Econometrics Journal 21:C1)",
    "doubleml"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 289,
   "method": "Single-index and varying-coefficient models",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/single_index_varying.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_single_index",
    "np_varying_coefficient"
   ],
   "when": "you want dimension reduction without full nonparametrics: a single linear index passed through an unknown link, or coefficients that vary smoothly with one modifier",
   "when_not": "the index restriction is untenable; the modifier is discrete with few values (use interactions instead)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "full kernel regression",
     "criterion": "no index structure is credible"
    },
    {
     "alt": "varying-coefficient model",
     "criterion": "the effect changes smoothly with a known modifier"
    },
    {
     "alt": "GAM",
     "criterion": "an additive structure fits better than an index"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "index_coef: the index direction, identified only up to scale and sign",
    "link: the estimated link function on a grid",
    "varying_coef: coefficient paths against the modifier, with bands",
    "bandwidth: the smoothing used for the link or the coefficient paths"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the index coefficients are identified only UP TO SCALE, so a normalisation is imposed and the raw magnitudes are not comparable across fits",
    "the sign of the index is arbitrary unless anchored; flipping it flips the link and leaves the fit identical",
    "varying-coefficient bands are pointwise and are especially wide where the modifier is sparse"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Ichimura 1993 (Journal of Econometrics 58:71)",
    "Hastie & Tibshirani 1993 (JRSS-B 55:757)",
    "Li & Racine 2007, ch.8"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 290,
   "method": "Generalised additive models with penalised splines",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/gam_penalised.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_gam_fit",
    "np_gam_partial"
   ],
   "when": "several covariates, each entering smoothly and additively, with the smoothness chosen by a penalty rather than by hand",
   "when_not": "strong interactions dominate and additivity is false; you need a single index (-> single index)",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "kernel regression",
     "criterion": "few covariates and no additive structure"
    },
    {
     "alt": "series regression",
     "criterion": "you want a plain regression on a fixed basis"
    },
    {
     "alt": "gradient boosting",
     "criterion": "prediction matters more than the shape of each term"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "fitted: the additive fit",
    "partial_effects: each smooth term on a grid, with a band -- the interpretable output",
    "edf: effective degrees of freedom per term, which is how wiggly each term ended up",
    "lambda: the selected penalty per term; gcv/aic for the fit"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "multi-line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "effective degrees of freedom near one means the term is essentially LINEAR -- that is a finding about the shape, not a failure",
    "the additive structure is an assumption; a term can look flat only because an omitted interaction absorbs its variation",
    "the bands are conditional on the selected penalty and do not include the uncertainty of selecting it"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Hastie & Tibshirani 1990, Generalized Additive Models",
    "Wood 2017, Generalized Additive Models: An Introduction, 2nd ed.",
    "pygam"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 291,
   "method": "Specification tests of a parametric null against a nonparametric alternative",
   "wrapper_file": "c33_nonparametric_semiparametric/specification_tests_np.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "np_specification_test"
   ],
   "when": "asking whether a parametric model is adequate, with a test whose alternative is 'some smooth function' rather than another parametric form",
   "when_not": "you are comparing two specific parametric models (-> non-nested tests); the sample is too small for a nonparametric alternative to have power",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "RESET test",
     "criterion": "a cheap parametric screen for neglected nonlinearity"
    },
    {
     "alt": "non-nested tests",
     "criterion": "the alternative is a specific rival model"
    },
    {
     "alt": "residual plots",
     "criterion": "you want diagnosis rather than a formal test"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "statistic, p_value: the specification test",
    "bootstrap_p: wild-bootstrap p-value, which is the one to report in small samples",
    "parametric_fit, nonparametric_fit: the two fits being compared"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the asymptotic null distribution is a poor approximation in small samples; the wild bootstrap version is the usable one",
    "rejection says the parametric form is inadequate; it does not say which nonparametric alternative is right",
    "power depends on the bandwidth of the alternative, so a non-rejection at one bandwidth is not a non-rejection"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Hardle & Mammen 1993 (Annals of Statistics 21:1926-1947)",
    "Zheng 1996 (Journal of Econometrics 75:263)",
    "Li & Racine 2007, ch.12"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  }
